course: biology
lesson: 296
title: Animals in the news: true stories, fakes and how to read them
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Animal stories are some of the most shared news in the world: a baby panda, a shark in a flooded street, a clever crow, orcas pushing boats. Some are true, some are old stories told again, and some are simply fakes.
explain: A news story is like a friend at school saying «you'll never believe what I saw»: it might be true, but you ask where they saw it, when, and whether anyone else saw it too.
explain: Good questions for any animal story are: who first reported it, is there a photo from the real place and day, do scientists say the same, and do the numbers mean what the headline says?
explain: Rare things make the news because they are rare. A shark bite is a headline, but the cows and dogs that hurt far more people each year almost never are.
fact: Zoos have escape plans, trained staff and alarms; most escapes are of small animals and end quickly, and people should stay away and let the keepers handle it.
fact: Scientists describe thousands of new species every year, mostly insects, plants and small sea animals, and each one needs a scientific name, a type specimen kept in a museum and a scientific paper.
fact: After almost every big flood a fake picture of a shark swimming in a street spreads online; it is usually the same old edited photo, and checking where it first appeared shows it is fake.
fact: New Caledonian crows bend wire into hooks and solve puzzles in several steps, and scientists film the tests so others can see it is real; crows can also recognise and remember people's faces.
fact: Since 2020 a small group of orcas near Spain and Portugal has been reported pushing and biting the rudders of sailing boats; scientists are not sure if it is play, a fashion in the group or something else, and no person has been hurt by them.
fact: Whales strand on beaches for many reasons, such as sickness, loud noise, confusing coastlines or a group following its leader; rescuers keep them wet and cool and try to float them back out to sea, and people should call experts rather than push a whale alone.
fact: Bird flu spreads among wild birds and has killed many seabirds and even sea lions; people should never touch dead or sick birds and should tell an adult, who can report them.
fact: A newborn giant panda is tiny, pink and blind and weighs about 100 grams, while its mother is about 900 times heavier, far more than for most mammals, whose babies (like a human baby or a puppy) are much bigger compared with their mothers; China lends pandas to zoos abroad as a sign of friendship.
fact: After people left the area around Chornobyl in 1986, wolves, elk, bison, lynx and wild horses moved in; radiation is still there, and scientists study how the animals live with it.
fact: The Asian giant hornet, called the murder hornet in headlines, attacks honeybee hives and can kill a whole hive; it reached North America in 2019; scientists tied tiny trackers to hornets, found and destroyed the nests, and the United States declared it wiped out there in 2024.
fact: Animal attacks on people are rare, and they make big news because they are rare; headlines can make a rare danger feel common.
fact: The Living Planet Index reported in 2024 that monitored wildlife populations had shrunk on average by about 73 percent since 1970; this is an average across groups of animals, not the number of animals or species lost, and some groups grew while many fell.
exercise: The news says a zoo animal has escaped near your street. What should you do? | Stay away and let the trained keepers handle it | Go out and try to catch it with a big fishing net | Follow it closely to take a good video for your friends | Leave food outside your door to tempt it back home
exercise: About how many new species do scientists describe every year? | Thousands, mostly insects, plants and small sea animals | About one a year, usually a big new mammal in a jungle | None, every species is already known | Millions, mostly new kinds of birds
exercise: What does a new species need before it is officially named? | A scientific name, a type specimen and a scientific paper | Only a clear photo posted online by the person who found it | A vote by the people of the country | A famous person to see it alive
exercise: A picture of a shark in a flooded street goes viral after a storm. What is it most likely to be? | An old edited photo that spreads after every flood | A real shark that swam all the way up from the sea | A dolphin that got lost in a garden | A giant fish that escaped from the local pond
exercise: How can you check whether an animal photo in the news is real? | Find where and when it first appeared | Count how many times it was shared online | See whether the colours look bright and sharp | Ask whether your friends liked it and shared it
exercise: What can New Caledonian crows do in tests? | Bend wire into hooks and solve puzzles in steps | Count to a thousand and read whole words out loud | Open any locked box with just their long tongues | Speak whole sentences in English, just like parrots
exercise: What have some orcas near Spain and Portugal been doing since 2020? | Pushing and biting the rudders of sailing boats | Attacking swimmers on crowded beaches every summer | Jumping onto boats to steal fish | Sinking ships so they can eat all the sailors
exercise: Which is a reason why whales strand on beaches? | Sickness, loud noise, confusing coasts or following a leader | They want to sunbathe on the warm sand for a while | They are looking for fresh drinking water in rivers and streams | They are trying to lay eggs on land
exercise: What do rescuers do for a stranded whale? | Keep it wet and cool and try to float it back | Cover it with dry towels to warm it up before the night | Pull it back into the sea by its tail straight away | Feed it buckets of fresh fish right there on the beach
exercise: You find a dead seabird on the beach during a bird flu outbreak. What should you do? | Not touch it, and tell an adult who can report it | Pick it up with your hands and bury it yourself in the sand | Take it home in a plastic bag to show your whole class | Give it to a hungry dog or a cat to eat for its dinner
exercise: About how heavy is a newborn giant panda? | About 100 grams, as light as a small apple | About 10 kilograms, as heavy as a small dog | About as heavy as its mother | About 1 gram, like a paper clip
exercise: Why do panda births in zoos get so much news? | Pandas are rare and loved, and China lends them as a sign of friendship | Pandas are born only once every hundred years | Baby pandas are born already black and white and walking about on their own | Zoos must pay a fine if they keep it secret
exercise: Which animals moved into the Chornobyl area after people left in 1986? | Wolves, elk, bison, lynx and wild horses | Lions, zebras and giraffes from a nearby zoo | Only rats and pigeons, which can live anywhere | No animals at all, the whole area is empty
exercise: How did scientists find the nests of Asian giant hornets in North America? | They tied tiny trackers to hornets and followed them | They waited for the hornets to sting someone and then followed | They used sniffer cats in the forest | They asked the hornets with sound recordings
exercise: Why does an animal attack become big news? | Because it is rare | Because it happens every day | Because animals want to be famous | Because it is always caused by sharks
exercise: What does «wildlife fell by about 73 percent since 1970» in the Living Planet report really mean? | Monitored populations shrank by that much on average | Three in four animal species on Earth have died out since 1970 | There are only 27 animals left of each kind | All animals are now 73 percent smaller
test: «shark swimming down the high street after the flood!!! 1M shares» What is the smart thing to do? | Check where the picture first came from before believing it | Believe it, because a million people can't all be wrong about it | Go down to the street to see the shark | Share it quickly before it is deleted
test: «a cow's more dangerous than a shark? yeah right, sharks are in every headline» What is true? | Cows and dogs hurt more people each year; shark bites are rare, so they make news | Sharks hurt far more people than cows, the news is right | Cows never hurt anyone, they're gentle | Sharks bite only in films, never in real seas
test: Count them: a news photo shows 3 orcas next to a boat. How many tails and how many dorsal fins on their backs? | 3 tails and 3 dorsal fins | 6 tails and 3 dorsal fins | 3 tails and 6 dorsal fins | 3 tails and 12 dorsal fins
test: In Kung Fu Panda, Po's dad is a goose. A real panda mother is about 900 times heavier than her newborn. Why is that surprising? | Most mammal babies are far bigger compared with their mothers | Pandas are the biggest animals on Earth, bigger than any whale | Baby pandas grow inside eggs | Panda mothers are as small as their babies
test: «there's a dead gull on the beach, imma poke it with a stick and bring it home» What is right? | No, leave it and tell an adult; bird flu can spread from sick or dead birds | Fine, dead birds carry no germs at all once they are cold | Fine, if you carry it in your hands | Fine, if you wash it in the sea first
test: A news story says «crow solves puzzle». What makes it most believable? | Scientists filmed the test so others can check it | It was posted by someone with many followers | The crow looked very clever in the photo | The headline used capital letters
test: What does the Chornobyl story suggest about people and nature? | When people leave, many wild animals return even with radiation still there | Radiation makes animals grow to giant size | Animals cannot live anywhere near people ever | Nature never comes back after people leave, even in a thousand years
test: «they found a NEW species?? surely there's nothing left to find» What is true? | Thousands are named every year, mostly small animals and plants | No, the last new species was found long ago, before your grandparents | Only new dinosaurs are ever found | New species appear only in films
test: Count them: a pod of 5 pilot whales strands on a beach. How many tails and how many flippers do the rescuers keep wet, if each whale has 2 flippers? | 5 tails and 10 flippers | 10 tails and 10 flippers | 5 tails and 5 flippers | 5 tails and 20 flippers
test: What would happen if the Asian giant hornet had not been found and stopped in North America? | It could have spread and killed many honeybees, as invaders often do | Nothing, hornets cannot live in cold places | It would have made more honey | It would have slowly turned into a friendly bee
test: «orcas are hunting sailors off spain, it's war» What is the best correction? | No person has been hurt; scientists are not sure why the orcas push rudders | Yes, orcas have eaten many sailors there, and the navy is fighting back | Yes, the orcas sink every boat they meet | No, it is only one dolphin
test: «wildlife down 73%, so 3 out of 4 animals are dead» Why should you read numbers in the news carefully? | It is an average of population changes, not a count of dead animals | Because news numbers are always made up | Because 73 is bigger than 100 | Because wild animals cannot be counted at all, so every number is a guess
exam: Identify the course of action consistent with established guidance where an animal has been reported as having escaped from a zoological garden. | The public should maintain a distance and permit trained keepers to recapture the animal | Members of the public should endeavour to capture the animal themselves | The animal should be pursued for the purpose of photography | Food should be placed in the street to attract the animal
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning photographs of sharks in flooded streets. | Such images are customarily edited pictures recirculated after successive floods | Such images invariably record genuine incursions of marine animals into towns | Such images depict dolphins mistaken for sharks | Such images are produced by the sharks' own cameras
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the figure of approximately seventy-three per cent published in the Living Planet Index of 2024? | It denotes the average decline across monitored wildlife populations since 1970 | It denotes the proportion of all species rendered extinct since 1970 | It denotes the reduction in the body size of animals since 1970 | It denotes the proportion of all wild animals now residing in zoological gardens
exam: Ascertain the principal reason for which reports of attacks by animals upon persons attract extensive coverage. | Such attacks are rare, and rare events are deemed newsworthy | Such attacks constitute the commonest cause of injury | Such attacks are invariably perpetrated by sharks | Such attacks are required by law to be reported in newspapers
